    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d721-a1">

                <pb facs="0712" n="1446" sameAs="frus:frus1895p2/pg1446"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure in No. 758.]</seg></opener>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Cumberbatch to Sir Philip Currie.</hi>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Erzeroum</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1895-12-16"><hi rendition="#italic">December 16,
                        1895</hi>.</date></dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 312.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: With reference to my dispatch No. 289, of
                    the 25th ultimo, I have the honor to report, for the information of the United
                    States minister, that Her Majesty’s vice consul at Yan informs me that Dr.
                    Reynolds has returned to that place, but that the American missionaries in
                    Bitlis have decided to remain in the latter town for the present, in the hope of
                    a change for the better, though their position is very unpleasant, especially
                    that of Mr. G. Knapp, who, though he has a strong guard on his premises, has not
                    been able to venture outside his house since the massacres, as it appears that
                    the vali and the Moslem population are still greatly excited against the
                    Christians, and especially against him.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">H. A. Cumberbatch.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
